{% set local_problem %}
<p>A lot of Albertville homes have fascia damage that homeowners don't even know about. Years of overflowing gutters, clogged downspouts, or leaky seams have sent water behind the gutters and into the fascia boards. Alabama's humidity means that wood stays wet and rots from the inside out.</p>

<p>You can't always see it. Paint covers up early damage. But push on the fascia — if it's soft or spongy, you've got rot. And that rot spreads. What starts as a small damaged section can work its way into your rafters and roof structure if you ignore it.</p>

<p>We see this constantly: homeowners who want new gutters but need fascia repair first. It's not an upsell — it's the only way to do the job right. New gutters on rotted wood are a waste of money.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>We start by inspecting your fascia thoroughly — not just a quick glance, but checking for soft spots, water damage, and rot that might be hidden under paint. We'll show you exactly what we find.</p>

<p>For damaged sections, we remove the old gutters, cut out the rotted wood, and install new fascia boards. We use quality primed lumber or composite materials that hold up to Alabama weather.</p>

<p>Then we install your new gutters on solid wood, properly attached and properly sloped. The system works right from day one, and the repair prevents the water damage that caused the problem in the first place.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set why_us %}
<p>We've been repairing fascia in Marshall County for over 20 years. We know what causes rot in this area, what materials hold up best, and how to do repairs that last.</p>

<p>We won't tell you fascia is damaged when it isn't, and we won't install gutters on fascia that's going to fail. That's not how we do business.</p>
{% endset %}
